  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "bottom label 'initial sequence'",
    "rank of eight single-element gray squares reading 5, 2, 4, 7, 1, 3, 2, 6 left to right",
    "four two-element gray boxes reading 2 5, 4 7, 1 3, 2 6",
    "two four-element gray boxes reading 2 4 5 7 and 1 2 3 6",
    "top wide gray box reading 1 2 2 3 4 5 6 7 with label 'sorted sequence' above it",
    "thick gray upward arrows linking each pair of child boxes to its parent merged box",
    "the caption 'merge' beside each converging arrow pair (seven merge sites total)",
    "box widths scaling with run length so runs visibly widen and halve in count per rank",
    "highlight overlay marking the active child pair, their merge arrow, and the destination box during step-through",
    "stability tags 2ᴸ (left-half origin) and 2ᴿ (right-half origin) on the two duplicate 2 values when the trace is on"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "mergeStep",
      "type": "state",
      "label": "Merge call",
      "states": ["leaves", "M1", "M2", "M3", "M4", "M5", "M6", "M7"],
      "default": "leaves",
      "teaches": "Stepping in post-order (all four singleton merges, then the two half merges, then the root merge) shows the recursion order: a parent run is only built once both child runs are already sorted, and run length doubles at every level"
    },
    {
      "id": "mergeCode",
      "type": "code_editor",
      "label": "Implement merge(left, right)",
      "entry_point": "merge(left, right)",
      "api": [
        { "op": "less_eq_left()", "does": "true when left's front element <= right's front element (<= on ties is what keeps the merge stable)" },
        { "op": "take_left()", "does": "append left's front element to the output run and advance left's front pointer — the only way to consume left" },
        { "op": "take_right()", "does": "append right's front element to the output run and advance right's front pointer" },
        { "op": "left_done()", "does": "true when left has no elements remaining" },
        { "op": "right_done()", "does": "true when right has no elements remaining" },
        { "op": "finish(side)", "does": "append all remaining elements of the named side ('left' or 'right') to the output in order" }
      ],
      "frame": "the two input runs drawn side by side with a front-pointer on each, plus the output run growing by exactly one element per recorded step, with the just-taken element and the comparison that chose it highlighted",
      "input": { "label": "Left run | Right run", "default": "2,4,5,7 | 1,2,3,6" },
      "sample_code": "function merge(left, right) {\n  while (!left_done() && !right_done()) {\n    if (less_eq_left()) { take_left(); }\n    else { take_right(); }\n  }\n  if (left_done()) { finish(\"right\"); }\n  else { finish(\"left\"); }\n}",
      "buggy_code": "function merge(left, right) {\n  while (!left_done() && !right_done()) {\n    // BUG: strict < breaks stability — on a tie the right element wins,\n    // so the right-half 2 is emitted before the left-half 2.\n    if (left_front() < right_front()) { take_left(); }\n    else { take_right(); }\n  }\n  if (left_done()) { finish(\"right\"); }\n  else { finish(\"left\"); }\n}",
      "success_check": "the output run is nondecreasing AND, with the stability trace on, the left-origin 2 (2ᴸ) appears before the right-origin 2 (2ᴿ)",
      "teaches": "The merge inner loop is just 'compare the two fronts, emit the smaller, advance that side, then drain the survivor' — and using <= (not <) on the comparison is precisely what preserves the original order of equal keys"
    },
    {
      "id": "stabilityTrace",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Stability trace",
      "options": ["Off", "On"],
      "default": "Off",
      "teaches": "Tagging the two equal 2s by their half of origin makes stability observable during the run: a correct (<=) merge keeps 2ᴸ ahead of 2ᴿ in the final row, while a strict-< merge swaps them"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The merge lattice",
      "narration": "Read bottom-up: eight singletons merge pairwise into sorted 2-runs, those into 4-runs, and finally into the one sorted 8-run. Each rank doubles the run length while halving the number of boxes.",
      "state": { "mergeStep": "leaves", "stabilityTrace": "Off" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Step the merge calls",
      "narration": "Advance the merge-call stepper in post-order. Watch the highlighted child pair, its 'merge' arrow, and the destination box: a parent run appears only after both children are already sorted.",
      "state": { "mergeStep": "M5", "stabilityTrace": "Off" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Run the merge routine",
      "narration": "On the headline merge (2 4 5 7 | 1 2 3 6), step the code frame by frame: each step compares the two fronts and appends the smaller, then drains whichever side survives.",
      "state": { "mergeStep": "M7", "stabilityTrace": "Off" }
    },
    {
      "title": "See stability",
      "narration": "Turn the stability trace on and rerun. The two equal 2s are tagged by origin; a <= comparison keeps 2ᴸ before 2ᴿ, while switching to the buggy strict-< version lets 2ᴿ jump ahead — stability lives entirely in that one comparison.",
      "state": { "mergeStep": "M7", "stabilityTrace": "On" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Merge sort's correctness comes from merging already-sorted runs, and its stability comes from a single detail in the inner loop: on a tie, take from the left run (use <=), which keeps equal keys in their original relative order.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Recreate the four-rank lattice of light-gray boxes with thin black borders; box width proportional to the number of elements so runs visibly widen upward while their count halves (8 → 4 → 2 → 1). Connect each parent to its two children with thick medium-gray upward arrows and place the italic caption 'merge' at each of the seven convergence points. Top box labeled 'sorted sequence' (1 2 2 3 4 5 6 7), bottom row labeled 'initial sequence' (5 2 4 7 1 3 2 6). For the step-through overlay, tint the active child pair, their arrow, and the destination box in a warm accent (e.g. amber) while dimming the rest. For the stability trace, render the two duplicate 2s with small superscript origin tags (2ᴸ in blue, 2ᴿ in orange) and carry those colors into the code-editor's output run so the order of the two 2s is legible frame by frame. Keep the code-editor panel docked beside the lattice, showing the two input runs with front pointers and the growing output row."
}

